<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Congress of Chilpancingo</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1813">1813</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Congress of Anáhuac</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Congress of Chilpancingo, or Congress of Anáhuac, was the first independent legislative assembly of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a>, convened by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jose-maria-morelos">José María Morelos</a> at Chilpancingo in September 1813. It formally declared independence from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> in November 1813 and, after moving under royalist pressure, adopted the Constitution of Apatzingán in 1814. It dispersed after the capture of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morelos">Morelos</a> in 1815.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/congress-of-chilpancingo.jpg" alt="Congress of Chilpancingo" width="320" height="138" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Hpav7 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Congreso_de_Chilpancingo.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/miguel-hidalgo-y-costilla">Miguel Hidalgo</a> had called for a National Congress in a manifesto of 15 December 1810, but his government at Guadalajara ended after the defeat at Calderón Bridge on 17 January 1811. On 19 August 1811 insurgent generals at Zitácuaro set up the Suprema Junta Nacional Americana under Ignacio López Rayón, which still claimed to defend the authority of Ferdinand VII. Driven out by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/felix-maria-calleja">Félix María Calleja</a> in January 1812 and divided between Rayón and those, like <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jose-maria-morelos">José María Morelos</a>, who wanted a clear break with the monarchy, the Junta was nearly dissolved by August 1813, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morelos">Morelos</a> called a congress of provincial representatives.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morelos">Morelos</a> raised Chilpancingo to the rank of a city, and the Congress was installed in its parish church of Santa María de la <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asuncion">Asunción</a>. Since full elections could not be held, it combined elected deputies such as José Manuel de Herrera with members of the old Junta and substitutes named by Morelos, among them Carlos María de Bustamante, José María Cos and Andrés Quintana Roo. In his opening address Morelos linked 14 September 1813 to the fall of Mexico-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tenochtitlan">Tenochtitlan</a> in 1521 and presented the Sentimientos de la Nación, a 23-point programme of independence, popular <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a>, division of powers, the end of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caste">castes</a>, and the Catholic religion alone. Offered the title of His Serene Highness, he chose to be called Servant of the Nation.</p><p>The Act of 6 November 1813, drafted mainly by Bustamante, declared the permanent end of dependence on the Spanish throne. Royalist pressure then made the Congress itinerant, and at Apatzingán it sanctioned the Constitution of 22 October 1814, which set up a republic with a strong legislature and a three-member executive, first held by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morelos">Morelos</a>, José María Liceaga and Cos.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>At Puruarán in July 1815 the Congress decreed the first national symbols: a seal with an eagle on a nopal holding a snake, and flags for war, parley and commerce. A letter asked President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-madison">James Madison</a> to recognise América Mexicana, but no recognition followed. On 6 September 1815, at Uruapan, the Congress created the Junta Subalterna, which outlived it as the insurgents' last organised government, moving between places such as Taretan, Ario and the fort of Jaujilla, until around 1820 it handed its authority to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vicente-guerrero">Vicente Guerrero</a> before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plan-of-iguala">Plan of Iguala</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Congress of Chilpancingo” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>José María Morelos continued the struggle, convening the <b>Congress of Chilpancingo</b> in 1813, until his capture and execution in 1815.</blockquote><a href="/empire/new-spain">New Spain · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li></ul></section>
